ge position of each sensor, we sum over the $20$ particle positions for that sensor and divide by the number of particles. For example, the average x position for player $A$ is \[ (x_1^A + x_2^A + \cdots + x_{20}^A)/20. \] Define the position vectors $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_{20}.$ The $20\times 2$ matrix whose rows are the positions $X_1, \ldots ,X_{20}$ has average position \[ M = \left[ \begin{array}{cc} (x_{11} + x_{21} + \cdots + x_{20\cdot1})/20 \\ (x^{y}_1 + x^{y}_2 + \cdots + x^{y}_{20})/20 \end{array} \right]. \] To find the covariance matrix, we need all of the points in relation to the average position. We can rearrange the matrix $M$ to \[ M = \left[ \begin{array}{cc} 1 & x_1 & \ldots & x_{20} \end{array} \right]. \] To this matrix, we add the average particle position to correspond to all particles being calculated from the same average: \[ \left[ \begin{array}{ccccc} 1+x_1 & x_1 & \ldots & x_{20} \\ 1+x_2 & x_2 & \ldots & x_{20} \\ \vdots & & & \vdots \\ 1+x_{20} & x_{20} & \ldots & x_{20} \end{array} \right]. \] Adding this to itself gives $\left[ \begin{array}{ccccc} 2(x_1 + 1) & 2x_1 & \cdots & 2x_{20} \\ 2(x_2 + 1) & x_2 & \vdots & x_{20} \\ \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\ 2(x_{20} + 1 & x_{20} & \ldots & x_{20} \end{array} \right].$ One more thing to deal with is the "average position - average position" matrix $\left[ \begin{array}{cccc} x_1 - 1 & x_2 - 1 & \cdots & x_{20} - 1 \end{array} \right],$ which we already calculated, and therefore can be subtract out: \[ \left[ \begin{array}{ccc} 1 \cdot (2x_1 + 1) - 2(x_1-1) & & \\ & \cdots & \\ & & 20\cdot(x_{20} + 1) - 2(x_{20} - 1) \end{array} \right] = \left[ \begin{array}{ccc} 14 & & \\ & \cdots & \\ 420 & \end{array} \right]. \] When multiplied by $\left[ \begin{array}{ccccc} ((.5,1), & ((.5,2), & \cdots & ((.5,20) \end{array} \right],$ one finds the covariance matrix is found to be, \[ \left[ \begin{array}{ccccc} 14 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\ 0 & \ldots & \cdots & 0 \\ 420 & \ldots & \ldots & 0 \end{a